   "text": "I read the molecule as these fragments joined in turn:\n\n- **Right-hand end:** a 6-(trifluoromethyl)picolinamide whose NH sits on C5 of a 2H-indazole. C6 of the indazole carries a C(CH3)2OH group, and N2 is bonded to a cyclohexane ring.\n- **Cyclohexane:** a 1,4-disubstituted ring with a wedge at the indazole-bearing carbon and a hash at the CH2-bearing carbon, which I read as trans. I wrote this as `[C@H]1CC[C@H](CC1)`; I worked out the @/@@ assignment by comparison with trans-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane, so it rests on that reasoning.\n- **Linker:** the cyclohexane CH2 is bonded to the nitrogen of a 2-azaspiro[3.3]heptane. The cyclobutane carbon of the spiro unit carries a CH, which bears two CH2 arms.\n- **First arm:** CH2\u2013NH attached to C4 of a pomalidomide-type isoindoline-1,3-dione, whose ring nitrogen bears a 2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl group.\n- **Second arm:** CH2 on the nitrogen of a piperidine, which is substituted at C4 by a para-phenylene.\n- **Far end:** the phenylene NH is bonded to C3 of a pyrazine. That pyrazine carries a carboxamide at C2 and a piperidin-1-yl group at C5.\n\nOnly the cyclohexane has drawn stereochemistry. The spiro-bearing CH and the dioxopiperidine carbon are drawn with plain bonds, so I gave them no stereo marks. The SMILES below was written fragment by fragment from the drawing; I did not run it through a parser.\n\nSMILES: O=C(Nc1cc2cn(nc2cc1C(C)(C)O)[C@H]1CC[C@H](CC1)CN1CC2(C1)CC(C2)C(CNc1cccc3c1C(=O)N(C1CCC(=O)NC1=O)C3=O)CN1CCC(CC1)c1ccc(Nc3nc(N4CCCCC4)cnc3C(N)=O)cc1)c1cccc(n1)C(F)(F)F"
